    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Individuals of the same species differ in reaction norms, responding to environmental cues in distinct ways. Reaction norms describe the range of phenotypes a single genotype can produce, depending on the environment. In this study, we investigate reaction norms in foraging behavior of red harvester ant colonies (Pogonomyrmex barbatus) in response to weather conditions. We aim to build on previous research that has suggested that both foraging intensity and foraging regulation are affected by weather (Gordon, Dektar, and Pinter-wollman 2013; Azc and Kovacs 2007). At a field site near Rodeo, NM, we compared data from 2 years on the foraging behavior of 4 colonies, and examined the relation of foraging behavior to temperature and humidity. We statistically examined the correlation between weather conditions and foraging rate by quantifying foraging behavior of each day using 4 metrics: average rate of outgoing foragers, maximum rate of outgoing foragers, total outgoing foragers, and the difference between outgoing and incoming foragers. We found that colonies varied in their foraging behavior relative to each other in the same weather conditions, and differed in their sensitivity to varying weather conditions. Exploring the behavioral plasticity of colonies as they integrate environmental cues helps us understand more about the regulation of collective behavior.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
